ack to around 1304 BCE and serves as a critical archaeological site for understanding trade networks in the Late Bronze Age. This vessel, approximately 15 meters long, was laden with an extraordinary cargo, including over 20 tons of raw materials such as copper ingots, tin, and glass beads, which highlights the sophisticated maritime commerce that existed in the Eastern Mediterranean. The ship is believed to have traveled from the copper-rich regions of Cyprus or the Levant, indicating the interconnectedness of ancient civilizations and their reliance on trade for economic sustenance.

